## Hilbert Polynomial of the Twisted Cubic

**Setup.** Let $C \subset \mathbb{P}^3$ be the twisted cubic, defined by the ideal
$$I_C = (XZ - Y^2,\; YW - Z^2,\; XW - YZ) \subset S = k[X,Y,Z,W].$$
Its homogeneous coordinate ring is $R_C = S/I_C$, and the Hilbert polynomial is
$$P_C(n) = \dim_k (R_C)_n \quad \text{for } n \gg 0.$$

**Step 1: Parametrize $C$.** The twisted cubic is the image of the Veronese-type embedding
$$\nu_3 : \mathbb{P}^1 \hookrightarrow \mathbb{P}^3, \qquad [s:t] \mapsto [s^3 : s^2 t : s t^2 : t^3] = [X:Y:Z:W].$$
This induces a surjection of graded rings
$$k[X,Y,Z,W] \twoheadrightarrow k[s^3, s^2 t, s t^2, t^3] \subset k[s,t],$$
sending $X \mapsto s^3$, $Y \mapsto s^2 t$, $Z \mapsto s t^2$, $W \mapsto t^3$. The kernel of this map is precisely $I_C$ (the three generators $XZ-Y^2$, $YW-Z^2$, $XW-YZ$ correspond to the relations $s^3 \cdot st^2 = (s^2 t)^2$, $s^2 t \cdot t^3 = (st^2)^2$, $s^3 \cdot t^3 = s^2 t \cdot st^2$). Hence
$$R_C \cong k[s^3, s^2 t, s t^2, t^3],$$
the **third Veronese subring** of $k[s,t]$.

**Step 2: Compute $\dim_k (R_C)_n$.** The degree-$n$ piece of $R_C$ consists of homogeneous forms of degree $3n$ in $s, t$ (since each generator has degree $3$ in $s,t$, and a product of $n$ generators has total degree $3n$). Conversely, every monomial $s^{3n-j}t^j$ for $0 \le j \le 3n$ arises as a product of $n$ generators (specifically $s^{3-j'}t^{j'}$-type factors combined appropriately), so
$$(R_C)_n = \{\text{homogeneous forms of degree } 3n \text{ in } s,t\}.$$
The dimension of the space of binary forms of degree $3n$ is $3n + 1$. Therefore
$$\dim_k (R_C)_n = 3n + 1.$$

**Step 3: Verify via degree and genus.** As a consistency check: $C$ is a smooth rational curve of degree $d = 3$ and (arithmetic) genus $g = 0$ in $\mathbb{P}^3$. For any nondegenerate curve in projective space, the Hilbert polynomial is
$$P_C(n) = d \cdot n + 1 - g = 3n + 1 - 0 = 3n + 1,$$
which agrees with the direct computation.

**Conclusion.** The Hilbert polynomial of the twisted cubic is

$$\boxed{P_C(n) = 3n + 1.}$$

### PROOF COMPLETE
